# CREATE OR REPLACE and triggers under MVCC, seen from more than one connection.
#
# A replacement writes a new version of the catalog entry on the replacing
# transaction. Until it commits every other connection keeps binding the old
# definition and reading the old rows; COMMIT hands the new version to the
# next snapshot, a snapshot taken before the commit keeps the old version, and
# ROLLBACK leaves the old version in place for everybody. A second writer of
# the same entry is refused on the spot with a write-write conflict rather than
# waiting on a lock the way PostgreSQL does.
#
# A trigger is an entry in its table's trigger set, so whether a DML statement
# fires it, and which body runs, is decided by the snapshot of the transaction
# running that statement. The rows a trigger writes belong to that transaction
# and share its fate.
#
# The default isolation is REPEATABLE READ: the snapshot is taken by the first
# statement after BEGIN that reads the database. A READ COMMITTED transaction's
# first write pins its catalog view, so it keeps the definitions it wrote
# against to the end.


# ===========================================================================
# VIEW
# ===========================================================================

statement ok
